# An Ubuntu MICROVM WITH NO DESKTOP: KERNEL, INITRAMFS, ROOT FILESYSTEM, SERIAL LOGIN.
#
# THE SAME SHAPE AS THE ALPINE, DEBIAN AND NIXOS MICROVMS: a kernel, an initramfs, and ONE FLAT ext4
# qcow2 with no partition table, booted with `-kernel` because a microvm has no firmware to read a
# boot sector.
# MEASURED: THIS IS THE ONLY VARIANT THAT DOES NOT NEED ITS INITRAMFS. `CONFIG_VIRTIO_MMIO`,
# `CONFIG_VIRTIO_BLK` and `CONFIG_EXT4_FS` are all `=y`, so with `-kernel` alone the guest prints
# `virtio_blk virtio0: [vda]` and mounts its root. The initramfs is still handed over, because every
# variant ships the same three files and the boot step should not special-case one of them.
# NOTHING HERE INSTALLS ubuntu. `.github/ubuntu-rootfs.sh` builds the three files; this walkthrough
# boots them and reads the console.
#
# THE ASSERTION IS THE SERIAL LINE, NOT A SCREEN. There is no framebuffer on this machine - a GPU
# needs PCIe, and `pcie=off` means there is none - so the recording is the "NO DISPLAY" card the
# recorder draws while a guest has no adapter.

walkthrough "ubuntu microvm - a root filesystem, a serial login, and no PCIe"
  resolution 1024x768
  recording_resolution 1024x768

  # THE THREE FILES THE CI STEP BUILDS, named here so the boot step can refer to them.
  needs ubuntu-vmlinuz ubuntu-initramfs ubuntu.qcow2

  # THE ROOT FILESYSTEM, BUILT BY THE CI STEP AND NOT INSTALLED BY THIS FILE.
  disk ubuntu.qcow2

  # `without a display` AND NOT `with a console`: this guest has no VGA adapter at all, which is the
  # honest shape for a machine with no PCIe.
  #
  # `rootfstype=ext4` IS NAMED BECAUSE THE INITRAMFS NEEDS TO AGREE WITH THE IMAGE. `console=ttyS0`
  # is the only place this guest can be read.
  boot as a microvm from kernel "ubuntu-vmlinuz" and initramfs "ubuntu-initramfs" with "console=ttyS0 root=/dev/vda rw rootfstype=ext4" without a display with memory 1024, waiting up to 180s

  # THE FIRST FACT: the kernel found the disk. Without `acpi=on` this never appears, and the guest
  # reaches `ALERT! /dev/vda does not exist` or a `VFS: Cannot open root device` panic instead.
  the console will say "vda"

  # THE SECOND: init finished and a login is waiting.
  the console will say "login"

  # TWENTY SECONDS OF A GUEST AT A PROMPT, so the recording is a machine that stayed up.
  wait 20s

  stop the machine by killing it, waiting up to 60s
